{#
After a drop on a folder.

Wraps the shared bulk answer rather than restating it: the rows leave, and the
client re-reads the list frame for the pager and the empty state — see
thread/status/_bulk.stream.html.twig, which holds the whole argument for why a
list-shaped action needs both halves. `leaves` is hard-coded true here because a
move is one by definition; there is no variant of this action that leaves the
conversation in the list it was dragged from.

Dropping something on the folder it is already in would make that a lie, and it
is prevented at the other end: the sidebar row for the list you are looking at
takes no drops. See mail--dnd.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL — the toast.

Every other bulk action is a button. Its label said what it would do before it
was pressed, and where the mail went is a place you can navigate to and look at.
A move is a drop, aimed by pointer at a sidebar row a couple of dozen pixels
tall, and it ends with the conversation gone from the list and no statement
anywhere about where it landed. Naming the folder is what turns "it vanished"
into "it is in Receipts".

No undo offered, deliberately. It would have to remember the label each
conversation came out of, which for a selection spanning folders is not one
value; and unlike a send there is no window in which the thing can still be
called back — the move has landed, and the way back is the folder now named on
screen.

Variables:
  count    how many conversations were moved
  threads  the conversations, so their rows can be removed
  label    the destination, for the toast
#}
{% include 'thread/status/_bulk.stream.html.twig' with {
    count:   count,
    threads: threads,
    leaves:  true,
} only %}

<turbo-stream action="append" target="toast-region">
    <template>
        {% include '_partials/_toast.html.twig' with {
            message: 'toast.moved'|trans({ '%folder%': label|label_name }),
        } only %}
    </template>
</turbo-stream>
